{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>A chip is a compact, <em>dynamic</em> affordance: it appears next to the
     content it acts on, does one small thing, and can change state without a
     save button. That is the difference from a
     <a href="buttons.html">button</a>, which is persistent and consistent
     across screens.</p>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You are <strong>filtering</strong> a collection — the M2 placement is
        directly above or below the thing being filtered (a table, a list).</li>
    <li>The user picks <strong>one of 2–5 visible options</strong> faster than a
        select would allow (choice chips).</li>
    <li>An action is <strong>contextual</strong> to the content beside it
        ("Add reminder" on a note), not a standing toolbar.</li>
    <li>You are showing an <strong>entity or tag</strong> the user entered
        (input chips — a person, a label, a place).</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The chip <strong>navigates</strong> to another screen — that is a link
        or a <a href="bottom-nav.html">tab</a>, and its selected state means
        "where am I", not "what did I choose".</li>
    <li>The set has <strong>one option</strong> — a filter or choice with a
        single chip is a pointless control; M2 lists this as a don't.</li>
    <li>You need a <strong>primary submit</strong> action — use a
        <code>fl-btn</code>; chips are secondary by nature.</li>
    <li>The set is long and unordered — more than a screenful of choices is a
        <a href="select.html">select</a> or a side-sheet filter list, not a
        chip wall.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-types">Types</h2>
  <p>Four kinds, and the <strong>element is the contract</strong> — the same
     base class, a different native control underneath. Pick by semantics, not
     by looks:</p>
  <table class="docs-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Kind</th><th>Element</th><th>State</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Filter</strong></td>
        <td><code>label</code> + <code>&lt;input type="checkbox"&gt;</code></td>
        <td>many selected</td>
        <td>Labels/amenities above a list or table; add the
            <code>fl-chip__check</code> slot</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Choice</strong></td>
        <td><code>label</code> + <code>&lt;input type="radio"&gt;</code></td>
        <td>one selected (shared <code>name</code>)</td>
        <td>Sort order, size, density — 2–5 options</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Action</strong></td>
        <td><code>&lt;button class="fl-chip"&gt;</code></td>
        <td>pressed only</td>
        <td>Contextual command under content ("Add reminder")</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Input</strong> (tag)</td>
        <td><code>span</code> + <code>fl-chip__remove</code></td>
        <td>presence</td>
        <td>Showing already-entered tags, with a remove button</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Filter and choice chips contain a real input, so keyboard, form payload
     and screen-reader announcements are the platform's — not a
     <code>role="button"</code> imitation. Action chips are real buttons. Tags
     are static text plus one real button.</p>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Input chips are display-only",
    text: "M2's input chip can also be an editor: typing text transforms into a chip, chips reorder by drag, and a chip can expand into a details overlay. Flint ships the tag form (thumbnail + text + remove) and leaves the editor to the app: a text field plus chips is the honest web composition."
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <div class="fl-chip-set">
    <span class="fl-chip fl-chip--input">
      <span class="fl-avatar fl-avatar--xs" aria-hidden="true">P</span>
      Portland
      <span class="fl-chip__remove" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </span>
    <label class="fl-chip">
      <input type="checkbox" class="fl-chip__input" checked>
      <span class="fl-chip__check" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      Washer / Dryer
    </label>
    <button class="fl-chip" type="button">
      <span class="fl-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      Add label
    </button>
  </div>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Container</strong> — 32px tall, one line, never wraps or
        resizes. <code>--fl-chip-radius</code> is a pill by default (M2:
        16dp on a 32dp chip).</li>
    <li><strong>Thumbnail (optional)</strong> — <code>.fl-avatar--xs</code>
        (24px) for a person, or an 18px <code>.fl-icon</code> for a
        place/thing.</li>
    <li><strong>Text</strong> — 14px, one line, sentence case. Truncate the
        content, never the chip's shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Check (filter only)</strong> — the <code>fl-chip__check</code>
        slot, revealed only while selected.</li>
    <li><strong>Remove (input only)</strong> — a 24px button with its own
        <code>aria-label</code>; the × is drawn by CSS.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The 32px chip is below the 48px touch minimum, so the <em>set</em> pays
     for it: <code>.fl-chip-set</code> adds 8px above and below and 16px
     between rows, which makes every chip a 48px target without growing the
     chip.</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <h3>State lives in the input</h3>
  <p>The chip is a label; the checkbox or radio inside owns the state. The
     selected treatment is a token assignment keyed off
     <code>:has(.fl-chip__input:checked)</code> — so clicking the text, the
     check or anywhere else on the pill toggles the same control, and the
     native keyboard model applies: <kbd>Space</kbd> toggles a filter,
     arrow keys move through a choice group.</p>
  <h3>Selection changes width</h3>
  <p>A selected filter chip reveals its check slot, which adds 18px plus the
     4px gap — the chip grows. That is the M2 behavior; don't reserve the
     space or shrink the label to compensate, and never resize the text.</p>
  <h3>Overflow is native scroll</h3>
  <p>When a set cannot wrap, use <code>.fl-chip-set--scroll</code>: one row,
     horizontal native scrolling with scroll-snap, no arrows and no
     JavaScript. The half-visible chip at the trailing edge is the "there is
     more" affordance — M2's scrollable-set guidance, minus a script.</p>
  <h3>Removing a tag is app code</h3>
  <p>The library ships the button, not the data model. Four lines of delegated
     author code are the whole handler:</p>
  {% set removeCode %}
document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  const remove = event.target.closest(".fl-chip__remove");
  if (remove) remove.closest(".fl-chip").remove();
});
{% endset %}
  {{ snippet(removeCode) }}
  <h3>Deliberately not shipped</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Drag to reorder</strong> chips between fields — pointer
        choreography with no keyboard equivalent; M2 documents it, the web
        doesn't need it for tags.</li>
    <li><strong>Text-to-chip transform</strong> and expandable chips — an
        editor surface, not a primitive (see the input-chip callout).</li>
    <li><strong>Swipe gestures</strong> of any kind — a chip is a tap target.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The input is the control.</strong> It is clipped to 1px, not
        <code>display:none</code>, so it stays tabbable and in the
        accessibility tree; the label supplies the accessible name
        ("Personal, checkbox, not checked").</li>
    <li><strong>Focus ring on the chip.</strong> The global ring would land on
        the 1px input; <code>:has(:focus-visible)</code> moves it to the pill
        instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Name the set.</strong> Wrap filters in
        <code>role="group"</code> with an <code>aria-label</code> ("Filter
        notes by label") and choices in <code>role="radiogroup"</code>; a
        <code>fieldset</code>/<code>legend</code> pair works too.</li>
    <li><strong>The check is decoration.</strong> It is
        <code>aria-hidden</code>; the checked state is already announced by
        the input.</li>
    <li><strong>The remove button is a button.</strong> Give it
        <code>aria-label="Remove Portland"</code> — "Remove" alone leaves the
        target ambiguous when several tags are read in sequence.</li>
    <li><strong>Touch target.</strong> 48px effective via the set's spacing
        (see Anatomy). The remove button's own box is 24px — the smallest
        target in the library, and the one place a miss is recoverable
        (removing is not destructive to data beyond the tag).</li>
    <li><strong>Works without JS.</strong> Toggling, the tint and the check
        are CSS + native inputs; the tint needs <code>:has()</code> (baseline
        2023) — without it, the chip is simply not tinted, the state is still
        announced by the control. Removing tags needs the author handler
        above.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

